Hey, i'm working on an embedded C++ project for an automotive ECU and i've run into a major issue. The system has a gradual RAM exhaustion that only shows up after about 4-5 hours of continuous operation. I've checked most of the obvious spots but i can't find the culprit. 

Here is a snippet of the main telemetry loop where i suspect the issue might be surfacing:

```cpp
void process_sensor_data(const SensorPacket& packet) {
    auto* data_buffer = new uint8_t[packet.size];
    std::memcpy(data_buffer, packet.raw_data, packet.size);

    // Process the telemetry
    TelemetryProcessor::update(data_buffer, packet.size);

    // Log the event
    Logger::log_event("Data processed", packet.timestamp);

    // TODO: Cleanup buffer
}

void run_telemetry_loop() {
    while (system_running) {
        SensorPacket packet = hardware_interface.receive();
        if (packet.is_valid()) {
            process_sensor_data(packet);
        }
    }
}
```

Can you give me a systematic way to track down where this memory is leaking? I can't use heavy tools like Valgrind because of the hardware constraints, so i need a more lightweight or architectural approach.

